592186198558 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $F$ is a source-free vector field\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ along any piecewise-smooth curve $C$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7922961-2d09-5acf-9285-b8bc18d81065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198558 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-1)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is source-free, the result ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ is guaranteed for closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198625 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198625 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198625 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198625 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4f/4f9cd7d9e173410de891756ee2ef260a16677629dacbfc56ab5e6e207a9e40e4.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2446c5e-a91d-56d6-9926-c800e872256d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198625 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199265 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199265 #edb/ref 1061 35868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199265 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c5a36f7-0cf5-56e0-9ad2-156e162e6abd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199265 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199318 #edb/ref 1061 44699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e2deeb1-79f4-5a70-897d-e88547dd9d33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199319 #edb/ref 1061 44701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199319 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4b926bf-0635-5002-9a4a-976c76662c96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199319 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199328 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199328 #edb/ref 1061 44869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199328 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b606814b-0712-51ca-ad68-9fac2299c246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199328 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199478 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwhich is defined on an open set $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ Recall that a function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIf a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y),$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ is called a **source-free vector field**. \n\nSource-free vector fields have some interesting properties, summarized below:\n\n- *The divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at any point of $D$ is zero:* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0\n$$\n\n- *The *flux* of $\\mathbf{F}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero:*\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0\n$$\n\n- *Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nStream functions for source-free vector fields play a similar role to potential functions for conservative vector fields. So it's worth comparing them side-by-side.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" colspan=\"2\">Source-Free</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"450\">Stream Function $\\psi$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Relationship between $\\psi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F$ is tangential to $\\psi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Divergence is zero\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Flux is zero along closed curves\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Flux integrals are path independent\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td width=\"30\"></td>\n<td>\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" colspan=\"2\">Conservative</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"450\">Potential Function $\\phi$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf i +  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Relationship between $\\phi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F$ is perpendicular to $\\phi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">(Scalar) curl is zero\n</td><td align=\"center\"> $\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Circulation is zero along closed curves\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}= 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Line integrals are path independent\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199478 #edb/ref 1065 7703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199478 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69005432-5588-5ac3-bf47-9dd734bb6953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199478 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199485 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's use what we know about source-free vector fields to calculate the flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\,\\mathbf{i}-2x\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nacross the curve $C,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d6/d6adda2809af5d49c6b3dd0e81eae913dcdf02ceec0ae919fda0011d8a3dad2b.png)\n\nFirst, notice that this is a source-free vector field, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across any closed curve is zero. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199485 #edb/ref 1065 7751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199485 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "447855fc-5a5c-5450-99a6-91de0ed2203b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199485 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429347 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429347 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: