6795 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516795 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516795 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516795 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0c/0c7d224dce33a51b94aa81c11ac4fae923c294e61372eb12d77e3656a0f61489.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516795 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "443c8318-6bcc-5f69-a92f-89796fbae407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516795 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516846 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516846 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516846 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516846 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/40/40e6a88e309f6ce5fece628a6d9bd66ffd00105edd3816fac04ae534ef7558b5.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(2,1)$ and $B(-1,-1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(4x+2y)\\,i-(8x+4y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bee8fa81-1bb7-5df3-bc77-fd80b7f5193e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516846 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-8x-4y))=4-4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/36/363ebd04d2629ed2b1b29ccab326e22737cf42e4c026d62910a50a1dd88e80f2.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x+2y=4(2-3t)+2(1-2t)=-16t+10, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-8x-4y=-8(2-3t)-4(1-2t)=32t-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)=-3, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-16t+10)\\cdot (-2)-(32t-20)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(32t-20)+(96t-60)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}128t-80\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[64{t}^{2}-80t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =64-80 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-16.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516848 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516848 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516848 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516848 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3f/3f84859100733c56d6381f6ae5c3f5fa55c693621190b3f921e3e1e8f850a4f9.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,-1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+(3-2y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(1-2t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516848 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c428bbdb-3474-5c6d-a12b-bbe7007eab5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516848 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-2y)=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5186fb872f03e73124d04c2e9ff07e77fb336bef121484a9ff1b697ea0cf8bcd.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2x=2(1-2t)=2-4t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =3-2y=3-2(1-2t)=1+4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-4t)\\cdot (-2)-(1+4t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-4t)-(1+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot {[t-4{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (1-4) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c9dee19-233e-57e9-803b-852c86658342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516887 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ef43ced-14e4-5f9d-8129-931a0d00fe17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516907 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5930e567-37c7-540d-81dd-7080ad2f8497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186516795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592